#### 2.1.2. Quantum Gates

Quantum gates, which are unitary matrix functions, modify the states of one or multiple qubits. Dependent on qubit technology, gates are implemented in various ways. For instance, superconducting qubits utilize microwave pulses ([Krantz et al. 2019](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2403.12979#bib.bib7)), while trapped ion and quantum dots employ laser pulses ([Benhelm et al. 2008](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2403.12979#bib.bib3); [Nguyen et al. 2012](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2403.12979#bib.bib13)). On the other hand, Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) qubits use radio frequency pulses. The speed at which these gates operate can differ significantly based on the technology, with photonic qubits working in picoseconds and NMR qubits taking up to several seconds ([Ladd et al. 2010](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2403.12979#bib.bib8)). Gates that affect a single qubit encompass the X (NOT) gate, H (Hadamard) gate, and rotational gates like RXR\_{X}/RYR\_{Y}/RZR\_{Z}, and the UU gate. Gates impacting multiple qubits consist of the CNOT (controlled-NOT) gate, Toffoli gate, controlled rotation gates, and the Peres gate ([Peres 1985](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2403.12979#bib.bib14)).

